{"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLNumberLevel","kind":"identifier"}],"platforms":["macOS"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","anchor":"overview","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"When polyphonic parts are involved, the ordering within a MusicXML document can differ from musical score","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"order. As an example, say we have a piano part in 4\/4 where within a single measure, all the notes on the top","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"staff are followed by all the notes on the bottom staff. In this example, each staff has a slur that starts on","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"beat 2 and stops on beat 3, and there is a third slur that goes from beat 1 of one staff to beat 4 of the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"other staff.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"In this situation, the two mid-measure slurs can use the same number because they do not overlap in MusicXML","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"document order, even though they do overlap in musical score order. Within the MusicXML document, the top","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"staff slur will both start and stop before the bottom staff slur starts and stops.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"If the cross-staff slur starts in the top staff and stops in the bottom staff, it will need a separate number","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"from the mid-measure slurs because it overlaps those slurs in MusicXML document order. However, if the cross-","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"staff slur starts in the bottom staff and stops in the top staff, all three slurs can use the same number.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"None of them overlap within the MusicXML document, even though they all overlap each other in the musical","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"score order. Within the MusicXML document, the start and stop of the top-staff slur will be followed by the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"stop and start of the cross-staff slur, followed by the start and stop of the bottom-staff slur.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"As this example demonstrates, a reading program should be prepared to handle cases where the number-levels","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"start and stop in an arbitrary order. Because the start and stop values refer to musical score order, a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"program may find the stopping point of an object earlier in the MusicXML document than it will find its","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"starting point.","type":"text"}]}]}],"abstract":[{"text":"Slurs, tuplets, and many other features can be concurrent and overlap within a single musical part. The","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"number-level entity distinguishes up to 16 concurrent objects of the same type when the objects overlap in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"MusicXML document order. Values greater than 6 are usually only needed for music with a large number of divisi","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"staves in a single part, or if there are more than 6 cross-staff arpeggios in a single measure. When a number-","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"level value is implied, the value is 1 by default.","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","sections":[],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel"},"metadata":{"symbolKind":"struct","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLNumberLevel","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"MXLNumberLevel","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLNumberLevel","kind":"identifier"}],"externalID":"s:7IvorMXL14MXLNumberLevelV","role":"symbol","modules":[{"name":"IvorMXL"}],"roleHeading":"Structure"},"topicSections":[{"title":"Initializers","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/init(uintValue:)"],"anchor":"Initializers"},{"title":"Instance Properties","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/uintValue"],"anchor":"Instance-Properties"},{"title":"Default Implementations","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/Comparable-Implementations","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/CustomStringConvertible-Implementations","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/Equatable-Implementations","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/Hashable-Implementations","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/UIntRepresentable-Implementations"],"anchor":"Default-Implementations"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL"]]},"relationshipsSections":[{"type":"conformsTo","title":"Conforms To","kind":"relationships","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SL","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s23CustomStringConvertibleP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/Se","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SE","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SQ","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s27ExpressibleByIntegerLiteralP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SH","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s8SendableP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s16SendableMetatypeP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/10XestiTools17UIntRepresentableP"]}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnumberlevel"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"references":{"doc://IvorMXL/SE":{"type":"unresolvable","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SE","title":"Swift.Encodable"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNumberLevel/Comparable-Im